@section disagg-roles-coordination Coordinating a role change

The application decides which node owns shared writes and coordinates elections, replication,
and the handoff between nodes. It asks each connection to adopt the appropriate role through
WT_CONNECTION::reconfigure with <code>disaggregated=(role=leader)</code> or
<code>disaggregated=(role=follower)</code>.

<b>Step-up</b> changes a follower into a leader; <b>step-down</b> changes a leader into a follower.
Reconfiguration performs the local storage-engine transition, not the election or replication
of changes to another node. The application must prevent competing shared writers and satisfy
the transition's transaction and checkpoint requirements.

These transitions are asymmetric. Promotion incorporates recent local changes into the shared
writer's state. Demotion switches to a checkpointed stable table view, retaining any later
changes in the ingest table. Reconfiguration coordinates these changes with ongoing engine
work; it does more than select a different destination for new writes.

@section disagg-roles-effects Effects across WiredTiger

The role applies to all shared state on the connection. The following responsibilities must change together:

| Area | Effect of the role |
|------|--------------------|
| Shared checkpoints | The leader writes the shared checkpoint; followers adopt it as a new base for reads. |
| Shared metadata | The leader populates shared metadata with schema changes during checkpointing; followers adopt the published schema with the checkpoint. Local schema state is distinct from shared publication. |
| Shared history store | The leader writes historical versions to shared storage. Followers read the history associated with their checkpoint. |
| Key-provider metadata | The leader persists key-provider metadata with shared checkpoints; followers read the published metadata. |
| Stable table views | Promotion switches from checkpoint views to live writable tables; demotion switches to read-only checkpoint views. |
| Eviction | A leader can reconcile shared pages. A follower reclaims covered ingest table content without writing it back to shared storage. |
| Background work | Checkpoint cleanup is leader-only work; the deferred checkpoint pickup server runs on followers. Promotion and demotion stop or restart these services. |

The table above shows how a role transition coordinates different parts of WiredTiger. See
@ref arch-disagg-layered for shared tables, @ref arch-hs for historical versions,
@ref arch-disagg-key-provider for key metadata, and @ref arch-eviction for page reclamation.

@section disagg-roles-transitions Role transitions

@htmlonly
<img src="disagg-roles.svg" alt="The leader writes shared checkpoints; the follower applies local
changes and adopts checkpoints. Step-up and step-down change these responsibilities." />
@endhtmlonly

@section disagg-roles-step-up Step-up

A promoted follower already has a shared checkpoint view and recent changes in its ingest
tables. Step-up adopts any pending checkpoint needed for promotion, creates missing stable
tables, and incorporates the remaining ingest table changes into the stable tables. After
successful reconfiguration, the connection can perform normal leader writes and produce shared
checkpoints from that base.

Promotion is not just permission to write the follower's existing view: checkpointed data and
local changes must become a consistent live shared tree. Transactions and positioned cursors
must not span step-up. The application must finish transactions and reset or close positioned
cursors before promotion; it can start new work after the transition completes.

@section disagg-roles-step-down Step-down

Step-down transfers shared-write responsibility without restarting the old leader. There are
two ways to prepare for the transition:

- <b>Blocking step-down:</b> the application stops and finishes write transactions, advances the
  stable timestamp to cover their changes, and takes the concluding checkpoint. Writes remain
  stopped until it reconfigures the connection as a follower. No step-down timestamp is set;
  the application must ensure that the checkpoint contains all changes being handed off.
- <b>Asynchronous step-down:</b> the application sets a step-down timestamp to define the
  concluding checkpoint's boundary. The connection remains a leader while retaining writes from
  transactions beginning after notification in its ingest tables. Their commit timestamps must
  be above the boundary. This allows writes to continue during checkpoint preparation and
  handoff; writers that straddle notification may need to roll back and retry.

In both cases, the application coordinates checkpoint handoff and finishes all write
transactions before reconfiguring the old leader as a follower. The demoting connection switches
to follower operations, reading stable tables through read-only views of the concluding
checkpoint and merging them with any newer changes in its local ingest tables.

Asynchronous preparation does not allow writers to run during or span final reconfiguration.
Read transactions that span it must have a read timestamp; cursor operations racing the change
can require rollback and retry. See WT_CONNECTION::set_timestamp for the cutoff API and
@ref error_handling for rollback handling.

*/
